# [SATP](../wg/satp.html)

## Summary
The Secure Asset Transfer Protocol (SATP) Working Group met at IETF 127 to review the status of its core specifications (`draft-ietf-satp-architecture`, `draft-ietf-satp-core`, and `draft-ietf-satp-usecases`), resolve outstanding review comments, and address transport layering questions. The working group voted against resetting the publication path of the core document to build a generic transport layer abstraction, opting instead to finalize targeted updates for HTTP/gRPC transport requirements and TLS channel binding. In addition, the group reviewed progress on the SATP Implementation Guide, pre-execution Stage 0 architecture, asset registries, and Self-Addressing Identifiers (SAID).

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group & Milestone Status
- Wes Hardaker opened the meeting, reviewing Note Well guidelines, data tracker sign-ins, and the priority focus on completing the three active working group drafts for IESG submission.
- Peter presented the [Document shepherd report of satp-architecture and satp-core](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-document-shepherd-report-of-satp-architecture-and-satp-core-01):
  - **`draft-ietf-satp-architecture`**: Review comments from GenArt, Ops, and Transport directorates are minor. The overdue Sec-Dir review will not block forward progress unless requested by the Security Area Directors.
  - **`draft-ietf-satp-usecases`**: Hyojin Song confirmed all outstanding comments will be resolved by the end of November.
  - **`draft-ietf-satp-core`**: Substantial comments remain regarding locking semantics, TLS channel binding, crash recovery, and transport definitions.

### SATP Architecture and Core Draft Updates
- Thomas Hardjono presented [Updates on SATP Architecture and Core drafts](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-updates-on-satp-architecture-and-core-drafts-00).
- **Locking Semantics**: Lock implementations vary widely across blockchains (e.g., smart contracts, hash time-locks). The specification will define gateway requirements ($G_1$ and $G_2$ must support asset locking, secure channels, and fast recovery) rather than detailing network-specific lock types.
- **Crash Recovery**: Thomas Hardjono, Kyle Rose, Andy Newton, Wes Hardaker, Claire Frossard, and Denis Avrilionis discussed how to handle crash recovery without a normative reference to an unapproved draft. Denis Avrilionis noted that point-to-point state transfers between immutable ledgers remain deterministic, allowing gateways to reconstruct state from ledger history. The WG agreed to remove explicit crash recovery protocol mechanisms from the core draft scope for now, document crash recovery as an operational/implementation dependency, and cover limitations in security considerations.

### SATP Core Review Issues & Transport Layering
- Martin Gfeller presented [SATP Core – Main Review Issues](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-satp-core-main-review-issues-00) addressing feedback from Kyle Rose.
- The presentation discussed whether to rearchitect `draft-ietf-satp-core` into an abstract transport substrate with separate concrete profiles (HTTPS, gRPC).
- Kyle Rose, Ned Smith, Wes Hardaker, and Area Director Andy Newton discussed the trade-offs. Rearchitecting the document into an abstract transport model would reset the document's path, requiring re-evaluation through Working Group and IETF Last Calls and holding all three core drafts.
- The working group held a formal session poll:
  - **Poll Question**: "Should the CORE document be rearchitected into a generic layer abstraction which means resetting it's path?"
  - **Results**: yes: 4, no: 9, no_opinion: 0 (total: 29)
- Based on the poll and discussion, the working group decided not to reset the document path. The authors will clarify transport requirements directly in the core text while retaining HTTP/gRPC appendix profiles.
- **TLS Channel Binding**: Martin Gfeller, Ned Smith, and Kyle Rose discussed binding application sessions to the underlying TLS channel. The group agreed to include a `SHOULD` requirement for TLS channel binding in `draft-ietf-satp-core`.

### SATP Implementation Guide Updates
- Hyojin Song presented [Updates on the SATP Implementation Guide](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-updates-on-the-satp-implementation-guide-00) for `draft-ietf-satp-implementation-guide`.
- The guide expands coverage beyond the core protocol to include Stage 0 setup flows, open-source implementations (e.g., Hyperledger Cacti, Corda), and preparations for remote interoperability testing at future IETF Hackathons.

### Stage 0 and Asset Registries
- Denis Avrilionis presented [IETF - SATP - Stage 0 and Registries](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-ietf-satp-stage-0-and-registries-00).
- Covered the pre-execution Stage 0 negotiation phase, Transfer Context creation, Tokenized Asset Records (TAR), and asset registry structures used to establish semantic, legal, and operational context prior to executing SATP stages 1–3.

### Self-Addressing Identifiers (SAID) for SAT Context
- Ned Smith presented [Self-Addressing Identifiers (SAID) for Secure Asset Transfer Context](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-satp-self-addressing-identifiers-said-for-secure-asset-transfer-context-00) regarding `draft-smith-satp-said-satp`.
- Described cryptographic content-addressing using SAID for asset transfer contexts, integration with Legal Entity Identifiers (LEI / vLEI), and detached signatures in COSE/JOSE formats.
- Area Director Andy Newton noted that rechartering to officially adopt Stage 0 and SAID work items can proceed once the primary three core documents pass IESG evaluation.

## Decisions and Action Items

### Decisions
1. **Core Document Path**: The working group voted **against** rearchitecting `draft-ietf-satp-core` into an abstract transport layer (Poll result: yes: 4, no: 9, no_opinion: 0).
2. **Crash Recovery**: Explicit crash recovery protocol requirements will be removed from `draft-ietf-satp-core` and documented as operational/gateway implementation assumptions and security considerations.
3. **TLS Channel Binding**: A `SHOULD` requirement for TLS channel binding will be added to `draft-ietf-satp-core`.

### Action Items
- **Authors (`draft-ietf-satp-core`, `draft-ietf-satp-architecture`)**: Revise gateway requirements, add TLS channel binding (`SHOULD`), update transport text, and submit updated drafts by January 1.
- **Authors (`draft-ietf-satp-usecases`)**: Resolve remaining comments and publish an updated draft by the end of November.
- **Chairs (Wes Hardaker, Claire Frossard)**: Schedule a Working Group Interim meeting in January to review revised drafts prior to IESG submission.

